---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Term from "../../components/Term.astro";

const platforms = [
  { os: "macOS", arch: "Apple Silicon (arm64)", asset: "aimark_darwin_arm64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "macOS", arch: "Intel (amd64)", asset: "aimark_darwin_amd64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "Linux", arch: "x86-64 (amd64)", asset: "aimark_linux_amd64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "Linux", arch: "ARM (arm64)", asset: "aimark_linux_arm64.tar.gz" },
  { os: "Windows", arch: "x86-64 (amd64)", asset: "aimark_windows_amd64.zip" },
  { os: "Windows", arch: "ARM (arm64)", asset: "aimark_windows_arm64.zip" },
];
const releasesUrl = "https://github.com/nsheaps/aiMark/releases/latest";
---

<Layout
  title="Install — aiMark"
  description="Install the aimark CLI in one command, then benchmark your first model in minutes."
>
  <div class="container container--narrow page">
    <h1>Install aiMark</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One small <Term id="inference">CLI</Term>, no account needed. Install it, point it at a model,
      and you'll have a submitted score in a few minutes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="curl">Option 1 — one-line install (macOS &amp; Linux)</h2>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://aimark.dev/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      The script detects your OS and CPU, downloads the right binary from GitHub Releases, verifies
      its checksum, and puts <code>aimark</code> on your PATH. You can
      <a href="/install.sh">read the script</a> before running it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="manual">Option 2 — manual download</h2>
    <p>
      Grab a prebuilt binary from <a href={releasesUrl}>GitHub Releases</a>, unpack it, and place
      it on your PATH:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">OS</th>
            <th scope="col">Architecture</th>
            <th scope="col">Asset</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            platforms.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{p.os}</td>
                <td>{p.arch}</td>
                <td>
                  <a href={releasesUrl} class="mono">
                    {p.asset}
                  </a>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">Checksums (<code>checksums.txt</code>) are published with every release.</p>

    <h2 id="brew">Option 3 — Homebrew</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="badge badge--info">coming soon</span> A Homebrew formula is on the way:
      <code>brew install aimark</code>. Until then, use the curl script or a manual download.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2 id="first-run">Your first benchmark</h2>
    <p>
      The walkthrough below uses <Term id="ollama">Ollama</Term> and a small local
      <Term id="model">model</Term> — the easiest starting point. If you don't have Ollama yet,
      install it from <a href="https://ollama.com">ollama.com</a> and pull a model with
      <code>ollama pull llama3.1:8b</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>1. See what you've got</h3>
    <pre><code>aimark detect</code></pre>
    <p>
      Detection discovers your hardware — CPU, RAM, GPU and <Term id="vram">VRAM</Term> (or
      <Term id="unified-memory">unified memory</Term> on a Mac) — and any installed
      <Term id="runtime">runtimes</Term>, like Ollama or <Term id="lm-studio">LM Studio</Term>.
      This becomes your run's <Term id="hardware-profile">hardware profile</Term>. If something
      can't be detected it degrades to "unknown" — it never blocks a run.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Run Sprint 1</h3>
    <pre><code>aimark run sprint-1 --target ollama:llama3.1:8b</code></pre>
    <p>
      This runs the Sprint 1 <Term id="suite">suite</Term> — ten short
      <Term id="prompt">prompts</Term> measuring interactive "chat feel" — against the
      <Term id="target">target</Term> model. You'll see live
      <Term id="tokens-per-second">tokens-per-second</Term> and
      <Term id="ttft">TTFT</Term> readouts while it works through 3
      <Term id="warmup">warmup</Term> passes and 20 measured
      <Term id="repetition">repetitions</Term>, then a big score card with your
      <Term id="composite-score">aiMark Score</Term> and
      <Term id="sub-score">sub-scores</Term>. Everything is saved to a local
      <Term id="result-file">result file</Term> — nothing is uploaded yet.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Submit your score</h3>
    <pre><code>aimark submit</code></pre>
    <p>
      Submission publishes the run to the public <Term id="leaderboard">leaderboard</Term> for its
      suite, version, and <Term id="track">track</Term>. No account required —
      <Term id="anonymous-submission">anonymous submissions</Term> get a
      <Term id="claim-token">claim token</Term> so you can attach the run to a GitHub profile later.
      The server re-checks the score, runs <Term id="plausibility-check">plausibility checks</Term>,
      and replies with a public URL for your run.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Benchmarking a hosted API instead?</strong> Use a hosted target like
      <code>--target anthropic:claude-...</code> with your own
      <Term id="api-key">API key</Term>. Hosted runs cost real money (suites are sized to roughly
      $1–2 per run) — <code>aimark run --estimate</code> prints the projected cost before anything
      is sent. Keys stay on your machine and never enter result files.
    </div>

    <h2 id="next">Where to next</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <a class="card" href="/leaderboard/sprint/1/">
        <h3>See the boards</h3>
        <p>Find your row, filter by model, and see how your hardware stacks up.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/docs/methodology/">
        <h3>Understand your score</h3>
        <p>What ~1000 points means, and how sub-scores roll up into the composite.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/docs/glossary/">
        <h3>Learn the language</h3>
        <p>Every term the CLI just threw at you, defined in plain English.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
